    "file_name": "XIN_ENG_20061103.0312.tml",
    "doc_text": "XIN_ENG_20061103.0312\n\nLOS ANGELES, Nov. 3 , 2006 (Xinhua)\n\nNigeria aims to export more gas to increase revenues\n\n\n\n\nNigeria aims to export more gas to increase revenues\n\nABUJA, Nov. 3 (Xinhua) -- The Nigerian government has planned to export more liquefied natural gas (LNG) to capture significant market shares in the world to increase the nation's revenues, a senior petroleum official said here on Friday.\n\nFunsho Kupolokun, group managing director of the Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation, told reporters that \"the strategy being adopted to capture the market will focus on growing the LNG market and gas export pipelines.\"\n\nHe said a project carried out by the Nigeria Liquefied Natural Gas Limited (NLNG) \"has attracted over 3.6 billion (U.S.) dollars revenues to the country,\" adding that part of the money had been reinvested for further growth and the country's gas sector.\n\nAccording to him, the NLNG will contribute a whopping 20 percent of Nigeria's gross domestic product (GDP) by 2007.\n\nKupolokun said Train 5 of the country's LNG project was commissioned in December 2005 and the Train 6 was slated for being commissioned in 2008, with the growth forecast for the NLNG capacity of about 30 million tons annually by 2012.\n\nHe said the West African Gas Pipeline project would be commissioned by the end of the first quarter of 2007.\n\nThe official also disclosed that two additional regional pipelines were under evaluation including the supply to Equatorial Guinea.",
